WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.130
Hank: Good morning, John.

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:05.560
Since 1980, the cost of college in the US
has more than tripled, rising faster even

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.060
than the cost of medical care.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:10.780
Around 70% of students in the US will graduate
with some amount of debt, and the average

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:17.420
amount for those students is $29,000, that's
$312 a month for ten years of student loan

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.420
payments.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.330
Now, there are some ways in which you can
pay less, and if you're interested in those,

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:23.380
I put a link in the description, but it's
an undeniable fact that the costs of education

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:27.410
in America have increased greatly and that
student loan debt is becoming a burden, so

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:31.410
what are colleges spending that extra money
on?

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:32.670
Well, it's complicated.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:36.300
Let's start with the fact that since 2008,
colleges have actually been spending less

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.300
money per student, but students have been
spending more money per student.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.300
How's that possible?

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.120
Well, you might remember than in 2008, there
was something of a financial crisis.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.410
State budget shrank, and so did some institutional
endowments, meaning that students paid more

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.410
and got less.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:53.870
That trend actually stretches back even a
bit farther, with the top amount being paid

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.280
per student by states happening in 2001.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.710
But of course, these costs have been increasing
since way before 2001, and colleges are spending

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.980
way more money than they used to, so what
do schools spend their money on?

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:07.860
Well, the first thing you think of is academic
instruction, and that is still the biggest

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.249
category of spending.

00:01:09.249 --> 00:01:13.020
College professors are expensive, and in some
cases, you're asking them to come teach when

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.119
they could be working in the private sector
for pharmaceutical companies or investment

00:01:16.119 --> 00:01:19.619
banks and making millions of dollars a year,
and you want those really highly qualified

00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:23.349
amazing professors teaching and so yeah, sometimes
they get paid a lot.

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:27.670
The average amount of money a college professor
makes is around $150,000, but some professors

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.719
make upwards of a half a million dollars a
year.

00:01:29.719 --> 00:01:33.069
But during the last 10 or 15 years, during
which the college costs literally doubled,

00:01:33.069 --> 00:01:36.170
the cost of instruction basically kept pace
with inflation.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:40.179
Part of the way schools have done that is
by now hiring on as many tenure track professors

00:01:40.179 --> 00:01:45.329
and having a ton of like, part-time and grad
student and adjunct professors who do not

00:01:45.329 --> 00:01:46.810
get paid very well at all.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:50.909
So the cost of instruction overall is definitely
not where this big bump came from.

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:55.200
The costs that have definitely increased a
lot are in a jumble of different categories

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.549
that different institutions track differently
and so are very difficult to tease out apart

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:02.939
from each other, but there has been some really
great and careful research done on this stuff,

00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:05.250
so here are the increases in a few categories.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:10.219
Operations and grounds-keeping, room and board,
general administration, academic support,

00:02:10.219 --> 00:02:14.569
and a nebulous thing which has increased more
than any other category among four year bachelor's

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:19.540
degree programs, "student services", which
includes everything from concerts to intramural

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.220
sports to marketing to tutors.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:25.819
It appears that what's driving this is, in
a word, sales.

00:02:25.819 --> 00:02:30.620
Colleges are acting more like businesses and
treating students more like customers.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:34.349
Schools compete with each other, and in a
world where the costs are really high anyway,

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:38.879
it starts to look like a little fuzzy when
a student's deciding between $310 payments

00:02:38.879 --> 00:02:40.440
for ten years and $340 payments.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.489
It's only $30 a month, and if one school has
really nice dorms, a well supported tutoring

00:02:45.489 --> 00:02:48.909
program, nice athletic fields, a super dope
climbing wall, a celebrity level professor,

00:02:48.909 --> 00:02:53.420
cushy mattresses, and local, organic produce
and cafeteria salad bar,, then both the parents,

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:56.810
who are probably footing a big hunk of the
bill, and prospective students are likely

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.500
to choose the more expensive option.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.099
Some of those things are worthwhile, others
don't have much use beyond, like, looking

00:03:02.099 --> 00:03:04.650
pretty cool on prospective student weekend.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:09.730
Building and running a 30,000 person institution
that has facilities that outpace the quite

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:11.510
nice country club is expensive.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:16.349
Over the last 10 years, non-academic employees
have been hired 50% faster than academic employees

00:03:16.349 --> 00:03:20.060
at colleges and universities, and top-level
administrators who are responsible for increasing

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:24.549
that enrollment and revenue so that there's
money for everybody to spend, their pay raises

00:03:24.549 --> 00:03:27.530
have been more than double that of academic
employees.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:31.400
The average school now has about one non-academic
employee for every 10 students.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:34.450
There are some private schools where that
ratio is more like 1 to 3.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:38.550
Now, the absolutely nutso thing about all
of this is that it still works, because even

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:42.939
at these juicily inflated prices, college
is still a good deal.

00:03:42.939 --> 00:03:46.340
Money spent on a Bachelor's, Associate's,
or Professional degree yields a higher return

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:50.260
than the stock market, even in dropping out
of college after 18 months has a higher yield

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:53.769
than the stock market, though not as high
as graduating, so in a way, colleges are just

00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:56.920
working their way up to costing as much as
the value they provide.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:01.239
But I think that we can all safely agree that
college should not be about maximizing revenue,

00:04:01.239 --> 00:04:03.040
especially for state schools.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:06.650
The question isn't really whether college
is a good deal, it's whether college could

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:07.659
be a better deal.

00:04:07.659 --> 00:04:11.040
It seems a little like the increase in spending
is kind of a natural outgrowth of a capitalist

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:14.790
society and people making decisions that benefit
them, at least in the short term, but it's

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:19.049
worth asking if treating America's students
like customers is, in the end, going to be

00:04:19.049 --> 00:04:21.100
a disservice to everyone.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.729
Maybe we shouldn't be selling students an
experience.

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:27.940
Maybe we should be providing them with as
much enrichment and, dare I say it, education

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:28.940
as possible.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:32.979
Because while it is important that college
is a sound monetary investment, it is also

00:04:32.979 --> 00:04:37.639
important, possibly more important, that students
get through our higher education system knowing

00:04:37.639 --> 00:04:42.770
more about the world and about themselves,
and I don't think that that has to be a monetary

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:46.560
thing, and I definitely don't think it needs
to cost $40,000 a year.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.210
I don't know, maybe I'm crazy.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.680
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

